{%- comment -%}
  The head of a panel below the root: the way out of the drawer, and the way
  back up one level.

  Two controls, not one. The circle closes the drawer entirely; the arrow goes
  back to the panel it came from. A visitor three levels deep needs both, and a
  single X that sometimes means "back" is the thing that makes a drawer feel
  like a maze.

  `back` is the panel to return to, passed in rather than worked out — the panel
  knows its own parent at the point it is drawn, and nothing has to reconstruct
  it later.
{%- endcomment -%}
<div class="hmd__head">
  <button class="hmd__close hmd__close--sub" type="button" data-hmd-close aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.close' | t }}">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 27 27" width="27" height="27" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
      <circle cx="13.5" cy="13.5" r="13.5" fill="#e6e6e6"/>
      <path d="M9.5 9.5l8 8M17.5 9.5l-8 8" stroke="#33363F" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round"/>
    </svg>
  </button>

  <div class="hmd__backrow">
    <button class="hmd__back" type="button" data-hmd-back="{{ back }}" aria-label="{{ back_label | default: 'Back' }}">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="16" height="20" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <path d="M16 10H4M9 5l-5 5 5 5" stroke="#33363F" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
      </svg>
    </button>
    <span class="hmd__backsep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <p class="hmd__title">{{ title | escape }}</p>
  </div>

  <span class="hmd__rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
</div>
